  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nUpper-level northeasterly flow has combined with a strong offshore \nsurface pressure gradient to produce moderate to locally strong \nSanta Ana winds. Thus far, the winds have been primarily out of the \nnortheast with the typically windy corridors north of San Diego \nCounty being more favored. Upper level winds will turn due east \nacross the region this morning as an upper cut-off low moves to the \nsouth. Concurrently, the strong surface high over the northern \nPlains will migrate southward over the next day. As a result, winds \nwill turn easterly and strengthen in favored zones (San Diego \nCounty, southern OC, and the eastern Inland Empire) and weaken \nslightly elsewhere.\n\nWind gusts today will peak around 50-65 mph along coastal slopes and \nadjacent foothills (locally higher for east-facing slopes and \ndownwind of the Banning Pass). For the valley, corridors of wind \ngusts 35-55 mph expected, especially in eastern San Diego County. \nWinds slowly weaken tonight into Saturday as the easterly flow aloft \nand the surface pressure gradient both weaken. Gusts on Saturday are \nexpected to peak around 40-55 mph along coastal slopes and foothills \n(locally higher for east-facing slopes. Corridors of gusts 30-45 mph \nwill continue for the valleys. Santa Ana winds weaken further \nSaturday night and diminish entirely by Sunday.\n\nElevated to near-critical fire weather conditions will continue \nthrough Saturday, though relative humidity values will be slightly \nhigher than they were on Thursday. Some influx of moisture from the \nsouth will keep RH values mainly above 15% through the remainder of \nthe event. In fact, there is a slight chance of light showers for \nsouthern San Diego County Saturday morning, though there is an even \nlower chance (5-10%) for any wetting rainfall.\n\nLooking forward to next week, the return of onshore flow on Monday \nwill bring cooler weather for the coastal basin. Patchy fog is \nexpected Mon/Tue mornings as the marine layer rebuilds itself. \nModels continue to become better aligned regarding the strength and \ntrack of a robust upper-low pressure system expected to swing \nthrough the region mid-next week. This will bring a widespread \ncooling trend starting next Tuesday and widespread precipitation \nnext Wed-Thu. Precipitation will likely remain light to moderate due \nto lack of any notable moisture source, though model guidance has \ntrended wetter since yesterday. There is still some disagreement on \nprecipitation amounts and extent of cooling at this time. Elevated \nwinds are also expected for mountains, deserts, and coastal waters. \nModel solutions diverge by next weekend, though the mean of the \nguidance leans slightly toward upper troughing.",
